{"id": "113292", "revid": "2282829", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=113292", "title": "Dasmari\u00f1as", "text": "Dasmari\u00f1as, or Dasma in short, is a large city in Cavite. It is at the center or the province, and at the crossroads of two major highways.\nUnderstand.\nDasmari\u00f1as is located approximately 27 km south of Manila, and part of the sprawl south of Metro Manila; uncontrolled suburban developments led to the present crowding situations and cacophonous traffic. no population data for this page\nHistory.\nThe city originated from the barrio (present-day barangay) of Tampus, then part of Imus. The Spanish colonial administrators formed the present city from a merger of smaller barrios namely Malagasang, Malinta, Nancaan (Langkaan, since divided into two barangays), Paliparan, Salacay and Salitran. The original town was named Tampus in 1866, until it was renamed as P\u00e9rez Dasmari\u00f1as in honor of Governor-General G\u00f3mez P\u00e9rez Dasmari\u00f1as at the same year. Dasmari\u00f1as was founded not by the petition of barrio dwellers, but by orders of the Spanish military and religious groups. A downtown area, or \"poblaci\u00f3n\" is founded between the barrios of Salitran and Nancaan (present-day Langkaan), and the historic Roman Catholic parish church dedicated to the Immaculate Conception was built.\nAs the Philippine Revolution erupted, Spanish town administrators ordered the execution of suspected Freemasons, and continued advances by Filipino revolutionaries led into the Battle of P\u00e9rez Dasmari\u00f1as, which led to 150 Filipino casualties, including generals Flaviano Yengco and Crispulo Aguinaldo. During the Philippine-American War, P\u00e9rez Dasmari\u00f1as was protected from American advances, but Americans eventually advanced and occupied the town. The new civil government renamed the town into the present name.\nDasmari\u00f1as also witnessed bloodshed during World War II, where advancing Japanese troops ravaged the municipality's barangays, imprisoning residents on the town's central parish church and killing males on each barangay. Since the Allied liberation in 1945 and Philippine independence in 1946, Dasmari\u00f1as grew fast with migrants settling in the newly rebuilt town. The Dasmari\u00f1as Bagong Bayan (DBB, literally \"Dasmari\u00f1as New Town\") relocation area, the largest social housing complex in the Philippines, further added the town's population during the Marcos regime. After two failed attempts to cityhood, Dasmari\u00f1as officially became a city in 2008.\nGet in.\nBy bus.\nDasma is served by two commuter bus routes emanating from the Para\u00f1aque Integrated Terminal Exchange (PITX), Route 28 PITX-Dasmari\u00f1as and Route 29 PITX-General Mariano Alvarez, yet only Route 28 is useful for visitors as Route 29 leads through the primarily residential areas of Salawag and Paliparan. Buses heading to Silang and Tagaytay also pass through southern Dasma, while those heading to Trece Martires and Indang turn right at Palapala Junction connecting Aguinaldo Highway and Governor\u2019s Drive.\nIn addition, Dasma is also served by point-to-point (P2P) express buses, all operated by MetroExpress Connect, using Volvo low-floor buses. Buses take a shortcut from Las Pi\u00f1as through a private road across undeveloped land up to the easterly barangay of Salawag, where they reenter the highways.\nBy taxi.\nYou can take a taxi from Ninoy Aquino International Airport () to the city. A trip takes 40-60 minutes depending on the traffic condition. It costs \u20b1400-1000 depending on your destination.\nYou can also take a taxi from Clark International Airport () to the city. A trip takes 2 to 4 hours depending on the traffic condition. It costs \u20b1900 to 2000 depending on your destination.\nBy jeepney.\nIt is possible to take a jeepney, but it is much slower, with more frequent stops. The important routes include those traversing the Aguinaldo Highway and Governor's Drive, with Palapala Junction serving as an important interchange between the two thoroughfares.\nGet around.\nThe easiest way of getting around the city is by tricycles. They will bring you to any part of the city within minutes. A trip costs \u20b120-60 depending on your destination.\nYou can also catch jeepneys which are common around the city. They run on fixed routes and you can hail and ride on any part of the route. A trip starts at \u20b113 depending on your destination.\nAnother way of getting around the city is by taxi. You can catch taxis on the Central Business District and it will bring you to any part of the city. A trip costs \u20b1200-600 depending on your destination.\nBuses can also be found on the city. Like jeepneys, they run on fixed routes and you can hail and ride on any part of the route. A trip costs \u20b120-100 depending on your destination.\nThe main highways in Dasmari\u00f1as are Aguinaldo Highway, which divides the city into two halves as it runs southward from Imus to Silang, and Governor's Drive, which has a junction with Aguinaldo Highway at Palapala, between SM and Robinsons Dasma, and runs west from Carmona towards Ternate.\nDo.\nGolfing is the main pastime of both tourists and residents. It is available at the Orchard Golf and Country Club.\nSleep.\nThere are many hotels and resorts in Dasmarinas.\nConnect.\nThe city's postal code is 4114."}
