{"id": "114383", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=114383", "title": "Los R\u00edos", "text": "Los R\u00edos, the Rivers Region of southern Chile has not only rivers but also lakes, mountains, volcanoes, virgin forests, ecological parks and ocean beaches.\nOther destinations.\nThere are many national parks and reserves in this part of the country, especially in the Andean region and the coastal cordillera.\nUnderstand.\nThe capital of the Region is Valdivia, one of Chile's most beautiful cities lying on the Calle River 17 km up river from the bay area. The bay is unexceptionally beautiful as well as historic. The presence of 3 important Spanish forts dating from the 1600s are restored and well maintained. The Spanish lost the forts to the Chilean revolutionary forces without firing a single cannon. \nHired by the Chileans Lord Cochrane led his ships into the bay area only to realize that the forts were invulnerable (except for one element.) Cochrane took his ships further down the coast and disembarked the men. Realizing the major fort of Corral had no rear wall protection from infantry, the naval commander organized his sailors into ground troops attacking the fort from the rear and easily took the fort.\nValdivia is likewise the site of the world\u00b4s worst recorded earthquake which occurred on May 20, 1960. A resulting tsunami took even more lives as the giant wave came in at an estimated 42 feet high, likewise causing destruction and death in Hawaii and Japan.\nThe city has excellent hotels, restaurants and a casino. Close to the city are national forests and wonderful ocean beaches. Boat tours are quite popular as well are boat and jet ski rentals.\nFurther inland are several lakes with options such as fishing, bed and breakfasts, hostels, hotels, camping and during the summer many local folklore festivals."}
{"id": "114389", "revid": "235382", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=114389", "title": "Turbat", "text": "Turbat is a town in the southwestern Balochistan province of Pakistan. It is situated on the left bank of the Kech River, which is a tributary to the Dasht River. The Makran Range to the north and east descends to coastal plains in the south.\nUnderstand.\nThe town is a marketplace for dates grown in the surrounding region and has a date-processing factory. Jowar (sorghum), barley, wheat, and rice are also grown, and livestock are raised.\nKoh-e-Murad (Persian: \u0643\u0648\u0647 \u0645\u0631\u0627\u062f\u200e) is a shrine located in Turbat. This is a sacred place for Zikris where they believe that Muhammad Jaunpuri, who they view as the Mahdi, meditated and offered prayers for a long time.\nThe followers of Zikri sect gather here annually on the night of 27th Ramadan for a ritual visit (Arabic: \u0632\u064a\u0627\u0631\u0629\u200e, Urdu/Persian Persian: \u0632\u064a\u0627\u0631\u062a\u200e) to this shrine.\nThe town has a special place in Baloch folklore and literature, it is the home of Punnu, hero of the romance of Sassi Punnun. The remains of Punnu's fort can still be seen at Turbat."}
{"id": "114397", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=114397", "title": "Spiritwood", "text": "Spiritwood is a small town in Saskatchewan, Canada with approximately 900 people (2011).\nUnderstand.\nSpiritwood is the largest community in the region, and functions as the major supply, service, and administrative headquarters for the trading area population which includes several First Nation (Aboriginal) reserves including Witchekan Lake, Big River and Pelican Lake.\nMixed farming predominates and consists primarily of grain production. A Spiritwood company with global connections that focuses on pig genetics, producing breeding stock and commercial swine, has roughly 50 employees. There is some forestry in the region north of Spiritwood.\nHistory.\nThe district began to be settled around 1911\u201312; however, growth in the area was slow until the coming of the railway in the late 1920s. The first settlers primarily engaged in ranching. The Spiritwood post office, which had been established in 1923, was named after Spiritwood Lake, North Dakota, the hometown of the first postmaster, Rupert J. Dumond. After the railway arrived, settlers of diverse origins poured into the district and many businesses were established.\nIt was the scene of the two deaths of RCMP officers in 2006, where after a 12-day manhunt, the suspect surrendered without incident.\nGet in.\nIt is 125 km west of Prince Albert and about 110 km northeast of North Battleford at the junction of Highway 3, Highway 24 and Highway 376.\nThere is no bus service available to Spiritwood.\nGet around.\nThe town is small enough to walk around, but you'll need a car for seeing the region.\nDo.\nSpiritwood is in the centre of an area that features six golf courses (three grass greens and three sand greens), six regional parks and 35 lakes, all within a 60-minute drive. The lakes have excellent fishing for perch, walleye, pike and trout. Its close proximity to an abundance of lakes and parks enhance the quality of life with camping, boating, fishing, golfing, hunting, cross country skiing, snowmobiling and hiking."}
{"id": "114400", "revid": "2183461", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=114400", "title": "Utah Valley", "text": "Utah Valley is in Utah's Wasatch Range. Most cities are between Utah Lake and the mountains, along the main I-15 corridor.\nGet around.\nA car is generally needed in Utah Valley. There is some public transportation (bus and very limited light rail). Sundance Resort in Provo Canyon is the only mountain area accessible by public transportation.\nStay safe.\nSome people water ski and fish on Utah Lake. Unfortunately, the water is very polluted, so be careful to avoid drinking any water if you fall in and think twice about eating fish caught from the lake."}
{"id": "114413", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=114413", "title": "Carterton", "text": "Carterton is a town in the Wairarapa district of Greater Wellington, New Zealand.\nGet in.\nBy road.\nCarterton is on State Highway 2, which runs north-east from Wellington to the Hawke's Bay. The highway runs through the middle of the town and forms the main street.\nDistance and times (non-stop):\nBy train.\nMetlink train services run between Wellington and Masterton, stopping at Carterton, five times daily on weekdays and twice daily on weekends each way. Travel time from Wellington to Carterton is 1hr 25mins. Tickets can be purchased at the station (if the office is open) or on board from the conductor; adult one-way $17, adult same-day return $22 (not available on services departing Carterton before 08:00)\nThe railway station is located at the intersection of Broadway and Wheatstone Street, a seven-minute walk from central Carterton.\nBy bus.\nA regular public bus service connects Carterton with Masterton to the north and Greytown, Featherston and Martinborough to the south (you may have to change bus at Featherston if heading to Martinborough)\nGet around.\nCarterton is flat but the township is elongated (4\u00a0km end to end). Walking and cycling around the town is fairly easy. However, with little local public transport, you would need a car to access most of the district outside the township.\nDo.\nCarterton is a short drive from a number of tramping tracks in the Tararua Ranges. There is great swimming in the local pools and many surrounding rivers.\nBuy.\nThere are numerous farmers markets in the area on weekends. Otherwise the shopping in Carterton is minimal and leans towards the essentials.\nEat.\nThere are a number of small caf\u00e9s in the area with Wild Oats being one of the most notable"}
{"id": "114418", "revid": "2248002", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=114418", "title": "Jefferson (North Carolina)", "text": "Jefferson is in northwest North Carolina. It is the county seat of Ashe County.\nGet in.\nThe primary route from the north is NC 16 from the Virginia state line to just east of town, where it intersects with US 221. Traffic from Boone and neighboring West Jefferson and other points south take US 221 into Jefferson.\nThe closest commercial airports are Douglas International in Charlotte, Piedmont Triad International in Greensboro, and Tri-Cities Regional Airport in Tennessee. All three are about a two-hour drive from Jefferson.\nThe town has no bus service. The closest Amtrak stations are in Charlotte and Greensboro, also a two-hour drive.\nGet around.\nA car is a must as there is no public transportation. A sidewalk runs from the old center of town to just below the county hospital, but walking is not feasible to most stores and restaurants.\nSee.\nMount Jefferson State Park dominates the landscape and is visible from practically anywhere in town.\nEat.\nWinner's Circle, in the Best Western Eldreth Inn, offers a popular weekday lunch special consisting of a main course and three sides that rotate depending on the day of the week. Also, breakfast, lunch and dinner daily; check hours of operation at Thanksgiving and around Christmas."}
{"id": "114435", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=114435", "title": "Wilno", "text": "\"This article is about the town in Canada. For the city in Lithuania, see Vilnius\"\nWilno is a small community in eastern Ontario. It is the heart of a large Polish settlement in the area. \"Wilno\" is the Polish name for what is now the Lithuanian city of Vilnius. Wilno is nestled in the rolling, picturesque terrain of the Madawaska valley which was largely shaped during the demise of the Laurentide ice sheet at the end of the last North American Ice Age.\nUnderstand.\nHistory.\nWilno is the oldest Polish settlement in Canada. The first European settlers in this area, circa 1858, were mainly of Kashubian origin from the then Prussian area of Poland. They are an integral part of what has been called the Kashubian diaspora. One of the reasons they chose this area to settle was the landscape which reminded them of their homes.\nAt one time, John Rudolphus Booth's Ottawa, Arnprior and Parry Sound Railway ran through the town mainly serving the lumber industry. The former train route has now been redeveloped into a recreational path. \nWilno's namesake was the city of Vilnius (Wilno in Polish), then in a Russian-occupied area of Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth, now capital of Lithuania, the birthplace of Reverend Ludwik Dembski, who was a prominent community spiritual leader and town founder.\nGet in.\nHighway 60 forms the main route in and out of town.\nGet around.\nThe town is small and everything is within walking distance. There is no public transportation.\nSee.\nIn the lower valley of the town there is a small area celebrating the Polish culture. To the east along Highway 60 is, built in 1875 to the first Kashubian and Polish settlers in the area. The landscaped grounds on top of a hill provide views over the surrounding landscape.\nBuy.\nThe Wilno Tavern sells Wilno t-shirts and hoodies.\nSleep.\nMore accommodation is available in Barry's Bay."}
{"id": "114438", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=114438", "title": "Lawson", "text": "Lawson is a town of 2,651 people (2021) in the Blue Mountains region near Sydney.\nGet in.\nLawson is near Hazelbrook and Bullaburra on the A32 road between Springwood and Wentworth Falls. \nBlue Mountains Transit route 690K runs from Springwood to Katoomba via Lawson railway station.\nSee.\nLawson is home to many bushwalking tracks ranging from easy to hard. Bushwalks include Adelina Falls, Cateract Falls and Echo Bluff. Walkers must stay on tracks and look for cliffs.\nDo.\nApart from bushwalks, Lawson is home to a skate park, BMX, a 50-m Olympic swimming pool and some small nature and play parks.\nBuy.\nLawson has a small supermarket, a hardware shop, a pub, a bakery, post office/newsagent to name a few. For better shopping go to Katoomba.\nDrink.\nLawson has one pub and one Bottle Shop located in the pub.\nSleep.\nLawson has one hotel and a few B&amp;Bs. Katoomba has more range."}
{"id": "114447", "revid": "2321054", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=114447", "title": "Auburn (Kentucky)", "text": "Auburn is a small town in the Caves and Lakes region of Kentucky.\nConnect.\nBy phone.\nMost telephone numbers in Auburn (and the rest of Logan County) consist of +1 270 plus a seven-digit number, but the region is now served by an overlay complex of two area codes, with +1 364 being the second. A local or in-state telephone call now requires all 10 digits of the local number be dialed (omitting just the leading +1 from a local landline call).\nSignage on many established businesses may still display the original seven-digit numbers; dial 270 before these if no area code is indicated."}
{"id": "114482", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=114482", "title": "Hermalle-sous-Huy", "text": "Hermalle-sous-Huy is in Wallonia (Belgium).\nThis village is in the middle of an important commercial, scientific, artistic, sports and touristic region, between Li\u00e8ge and Huy, near Bierset and Tihange, and very easy to access, at 500 m from the N90 Li\u00e8ge-Huy motorway. Hermalle includes nearly its castle and its monumental farm, a complex of elegant houses of the 17th and 18th centuries.\nUnderstand.\nLocated along the Meuse (important European river), Hermalle is also at the foot of the Ardennes and therefore offers interesting differences in height for hikers and cyclists.\nIn time past important village of the region, fief of the episcopal court of Li\u00e8ge with its own court and vassals, Hermalle has escaped the industrialization of the nineteenth century and maintains a countryside charm.\nHermalle, which is on the right bank of the Meuse, is included since 1977 in the Engis entity whose administrative centre is on the left bank of the river.\nGet in.\nThe nearest airport is Li\u00e8ge-Bierset.\nEngis station is served only by slow trains. It is easier to go by train to Li\u00e8ge and Huy then taking a bus TEC line 9 (Op\u00e9ra - Seraing - Engihoul - Fl\u00f4ne - Tihange - Huy). Get off at \"Place de Hermalle\".\nBy car:\nThe nearest motorway is E42. Choose Exit 6 (Villers-le-Bouillet/Amay) if you go to Li\u00e8ge, exit 4 (Fl\u00e9malle) if you comes from Li\u00e8ge, to join the national road 90 and leave this road at the exit \"Pont de Hermalle\".\nOn foot or by bike, take the RAVeL 1, along the Meuse, to easily reach the village which lies also on the Great Hiking Trail 576.\nDo.\nOn weekend, visit the \"Ferme castrale\"[dead link]; there is a second-hand books and flea market, a tavern to eat as before, etc. From the courtyard, you can see 17th century castle (private property) - mosan style. The \"Ferme castrale\" is open on Saturday from 14:00, Sunday from 11:00 and it closes at 19:00 (November to February: 18:00). It can be visited on weekdays, by appointment, for groups of minimum 15 persons.\nOn appointment, you can visit (guided tour), in the Ferme castrale, the Gourmet museum[dead link] (history of food and cooking) and the museum \"Postes restantes\" (history of writing and the Post Office).\nOn appointment too, and only for searchers, the Goumet library, one of the largest gastronomic library in Europe with more than 20,000 books, can be open for searching information on food and drink.\nEnjoy walks in Hermalle, Clermont-sous-Huy and Engis: the country is really superb.\nEat and drink.\n\"Al Raw\u00e8te\", the tavern of the museums, in the Ferme castrale, offers the weekend for individual visitors a restoration based on the old and regional cooking (the oldest recipe offered comes from the fifth century). The price of dishes goes from \u20ac3.5 to \u20ac17. The tavern can be open weekdays by appointment for groups of minimum 15 persons.\nSleep.\nHermalle offers only 1 B&amp;B, but 5 comfortable country g\u00eetes are nearby, in the village of Clermont-sous-Huy."}
{"id": "114500", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=114500", "title": "Gyenesdi\u00e1s", "text": "Gyenesdi\u00e1s is a lovely resort village only 100 km from the western border of Hungary, in the West Balaton region 15 km from the H\u00e9v\u00edz-Balaton Airport. The towns of Keszthely, known for its fascinating Festetics Palace, and H\u00e9v\u00edz with its world famous spa are only a few kilometres from here.\nUnderstand.\nGyenesdi\u00e1s \u2013 with its 4000 inhabitants \u2013 is the perfect holiday resort especially for those who want to spend their holiday at the Lake Balaton actively and joyfully. Gyenesdi\u00e1s has a lot to offer for every age and interest from relaxation to entertainment and a wide variety of recreation.\nGet in.\nBy train.\nTrains are available in every two hours from Kaposv\u00e1r to Gy\u0151r via Fony\u00f3d (junction point to Si\u00f3fok), Balatonszentgy\u00f6rgy (junction point to Nagykanizsa), Keszthely, Tapolca, J\u00e1nosh\u00e1za (junction point to Veszpr\u00e9m and Zalaegerszeg), Celld\u00f6m\u00f6lk (junction point to Szombathely) and P\u00e1pa. The journey from Keszthely takes 5 minutes and costs. From Budapest, you have to transfer in Balatonszentgy\u00f6rgy. The whole journey from Budapest takes less than 3 hours and costs.\nAt the summer, direct trains are available from P\u00e9cs and Szombathely.\nBy bus.\nBuses are available from Budapest-N\u00e9pliget five times a day. The journey takes 3\u00bd hours and costs. More frequent buses are available from Keszthely. The journey takes 10\u201315 minutes and costs.\nGet around.\nThere is no local public transport or taxi service. You can use the regional buses operated by Vol\u00e1nbusz.\nBy bike.\nBalaton Bicycle Road is a good way explore the beaches and other attractions in the area.\nThere is a bike rental. Bikes can be pick up and hand down at Tourinform Gyenesdi\u00e1s. Bike rental: for three hours, for one day (24 hrs).\nSee.\nFurther afield.\nIn Rezi:\nDo.\nBeaches.\nThere are two beaches available for the lovers of bathing, swimming and sunshine.\nEat.\nHungarian cuisine with famous wines. Family-friendly restaurants provide for the kids, so that everybody can enjoy their lunch.\nSleep.\nPrivate accommodation have a tradition for approximately 30 years in the village, but tourism has a history of 100 years. Hotel, B&amp;B places with their own swimming pool and different wellness services are here, as well as over half hundred private accommodation.\nConnect.\nGyenesdi\u00e1s' postal code is H-8315, and its telephone area code is 83."}
{"id": "114510", "revid": "124323", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=114510", "title": "Mahanandi", "text": "Mahanandi (Telugu: \u0c2e\u0c39\u0c3e\u0c28\u0c02\u0c26\u0c3f meaning Majestic Cow) is a picturesque village surrounded by thick forests east of the Nallamala Hills near Nandyal, Kurnool District, Andhra Pradesh, India. \nWithin 15 km of Mahanandi, there are nine Nandi shrines known as Nava Nandis. Mahanandi is one of the Nava Nandis. The Mahanandiswara Swamy Temple, an important shrine, is located here. This ancient temple dates back over 1,500 years. The inscriptions of 10th century tablets speak of the temple being repaired and rebuilt several times.\nThese nine temples are Mahanandi, Shivanandi, Vinayakanandi, Somanandi, Prathamanandi, Garudanandi, Suryanandi, Krishnanandi (also called Vishnunandi) and Naganandi.\nA festival is held here annually during February and March to celebrate Maha Shivaratri, the Great Night of Shiva.\nGet in.\nMahanandi is about 14 km from Nandyal Bus Stop. The nearest airport is at Hyderabad, which is about 215 km from Kurnool, and the nearest railway station is at Nandyal.\nSleep.\nThere are several government and private lodges near the temple premises, offering decent accommodation and food for visitors. These lodges provide a comfortable stay for pilgrims and tourists alike."}
{"id": "114524", "revid": "107092", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=114524", "title": "Pontivy", "text": "Pontivy is in Morbihan, Brittany located in north-west France."}
{"id": "114534", "revid": "144844", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=114534", "title": "Bargarh", "text": "Bargarh is a town and a municipality in Bargarh district in the state of Odisha, India. It is the administrative headquarters of Bargarh District.\nGet in.\nBargarh is well-connected to major cities in India like Kolkata, Chennai, Bangalore, Mumbai, Hyderabad and Ahmadabad. \nIt is also well connected by road to major cities around it with night buses to and from Bhubaneswar, Bhilai, Rourkela, Raipur and Cuttuck."}
{"id": "114540", "revid": "1806", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=114540", "title": "Adairville", "text": "Adairville is in Kentucky.\nConnect.\nBy phone.\nMost telephone numbers in Adairville and the rest of Logan County consist of +1 270 plus a seven-digit number, but the region is now served by an overlay complex of two area codes, with +1 364 being the second. A local or in-state telephone call now requires all 10 digits of the local number be dialed (omitting just the leading +1 from a local landline call).\nSignage on many established businesses may still display the original seven-digit numbers; dial 270 before these if no area code is indicated."}
{"id": "114541", "revid": "2256076", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=114541", "title": "Muzhakkunnu", "text": "Muzhakkunnu is a village located in the valley of Puralimala (Purali hills) in Kannur District in the Indian state of Kerala. This is a historical place famous from ancient days.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nThe nearest airports are Kozhikode and Mangalore. Upcoming Kannur International Airport is just 15 km away.\nBy road.\nIt is 42 km from both Thalassery and Kannur, and 55 km from Thaliparambu. The route from Thalasserry goes through Koothuparanba towards Mattanur- take a right turn at Uruvachal junction, about 4 km before Mattanur. A ride of 14 km through this road via Thillankerry will take you to Muzhakunnu. The temple is about 200 meters away.\nFrom Kannur move to Mattanur from here to Uliyil (about 9 km). From Uliyil town take a right turn to reach Thillankerry, and then to Muzhakunnu town."}
{"id": "114545", "revid": "235382", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=114545", "title": "Clayton (Georgia)", "text": "Clayton is a city in the very north-east corner of Georgia. It is the county seat of Rabun County. no population data for this page\nUnderstand.\nRabun County was founded in 1829 and named after the present Governor of Georgia, William Rabun. The area that eventually became Clayton was called the Dividings because it sat at the intersection of three crucial Cherokee trails. Claytonsville was founded here by European-American settlers in 1821 as the seat of Rabun County. It was named after a prominent jurist and congressman, Judge Augustin S. Clayton. In 1823, the town was incorporated and renamed Clayton.\nGet in.\nFrom Atlanta, take I-85 N for 28 miles to I-985 N which continues onto US-23 N after 24 miles. Continue 52 miles on US-23 N directly into Clayton.\nFrom Knoxville, head on I-40 for 92 miles, take exit 27 to get on US-74. After 26 miles, merge onto US-23 S toward US-441. Continue on to the ramp after 21 miles for another 21 miles to reach Clayton."}
{"id": "114554", "revid": "2236161", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=114554", "title": "Pound Ridge", "text": "Pound Ridge is in Westchester County.\nUnderstand.\nPound Ridge is a small town on the border of Connecticut, known in the area for its parks and nature preserves.\nGet in.\nThere is no public transportation in Pound Ridge so a car is necessary. \nCommonly used train stations in the area include:\nGet around.\nWithin Pound Ridge there is no system of public transportation so most people use cars. Most roads have little traffic so they are nice roads to bike on, but to get anywhere you have to cover large distances.\nSee.\nNature Preserves\nPound Ridge Museum - run by the historical society\nDo.\n\"Dance &amp; Fitness\"\nEat.\nIn Scott's Corners\nSleep.\nThere are no hotels in Pound Ridge; most people stay in Stamford, Connecticut or make a day trip from New York City. There is also an inn in nearby Bedford."}
{"id": "114557", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=114557", "title": "Husseren-les-Ch\u00e2teaux", "text": "Husseren-les-Ch\u00e2teaux is in the foothills of the Vosges mountains, 6 km south of Colmar in the heart of Alsace. It is the highest wine village on the Alsatian Vineyard Route.\nUnderstand.\nThe village of Husseren les Ch\u00e2teaux counts a little more than 400 inhabitants of which more than twenty families are independent wine-growers. The small streets, the church (its statue of Saint Pancrace XIV and its Rinkenback org from 1890) and the typical half-timbered houses constitute a nice little walk ending in the vineyards or the forest, it all offering a splendid panoramic view on the Alsatian vineyard, open country and the Black Forest.\nHusseren-les-Ch\u00e2teaux is named after the ruins of the castles on the top of the mountain. The village contains a hotel resort with an \u00e0 la carte restaurant.\nGet in.\nThe town can be reached by taking the departmental road 83 at the western exit of Colmar. \nComing from Mulhouse from the A35 motorway, take exit 28 towards Niederhergheim, Herrlisheim, Munster, G\u00e9rardmer. Follow the D 1bis to the RN 83, then take the Eguisheim exit, then Husseren-les-Ch\u00e2teaux.\nComing from Strasbourg, take the A35 then the RN 83. Then take exit 23 towards Colmar, Houssen, Munster. Follow the RN 83 towards Belfort and take the Eguisheim exit, then Husseren-les-Ch\u00e2teaux.\nDo.\nVisit one of several wineries in or near the village:"}
{"id": "114560", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=114560", "title": "Burton (Texas)", "text": "Burton is a small rural town on the western end of Washington County (Texas) in the state of Texas. Built on the traditions of German immigrants, Burton still holds true to its small town values. You will be hard to find an unfriendly face or an unpleasant greeting. Although Burton is in the middle of it all - minutes from Washington on the Brazos, a couple hours from The Alamo, an hour and half from the State Capital, and a few hours from the coast - the citizens still keep to a humble small-town life that is rare to find.\nUnderstand.\nBurton was established in 1862 and named for John M. Burton, an early settler in the area. The town is a rural, farming, German community with deep heritage. The town's economy benefited from the extension of the Houston and Texas Central Railway from Brenham to Austin after the Civil War. During the 1880s Burton had three churches, a bank, a school, and a hotel, and by 1885 it had a population of 150. Burton had a population of 800 and thirty businesses by the mid-1940s. During the 1970s, however, the town declined, and its residents had dwindled to 311 by 1990.\nGet in.\nUS Hwy 290 runs straight through Burton. You can either enter eastbound on 290 coming from Austin or westbound coming from Houston. You may travel down FM 1697 or FM 390 to come in on the Northern side of town. Both routes offer plenty of historical markers for understanding the historical significance of the area. If you are traveling by private plane, Brenham has the closest municipal airport, and is approximately 20 miles away.\nGet around.\nAll modes of transportation are accommodated to the streets of Burton. You can easily get around in a car or bicycle, but it due to its size walking is a great alternative. You might even find a group of horseback riders out for a ride.\nDrink.\nEven though Burton is a small town, but you can still find places to wet your whistle. Keeping true to its German heritage, a cold beer is always a great way to spend an evening catching up."}
{"id": "114647", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=114647", "title": "Mormon Lake Village", "text": "Mormon Lake Village is a place in Arizona.\nUnderstand.\nIt was settled during years when the nearby Mormon Lake was full, and many homes have docks that are very far from the shoreline. During dry years, the lake becomes a shallow marsh."}
